      ":question/id": "4a734f55-ceac-587c-a831-fa8bed578a99",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "e-148",
      ":question/problem": "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y = 2x^3 -x^2$ at the point $(1,1).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x) = 2x^3-x^2.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\nf'(x)  =6x^2 -2x\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nm=f'(1) = 6(1)^2 - 2(1) = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line $m'$ is\n\n$$\nm' = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac 1 4.\n$$"
    },
