        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-342142",
        ":question/problem": "Find the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line to the curve $y=4-3{x}^{2}$ at the point $(1,1).$\n\n${m}^{'}=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=4-3{x}^{2}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4-3{x}^{2})=-6x\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(1)=-6(1)=-6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{'} & =-\\frac{1}{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(-6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
