        ":question/problem": "Find the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{5}-4{x}^{2}$ at the point $(2,-8).$\n\n${m}^{'}=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{5}-4{x}^{2}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{5}-4{x}^{2})=\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{4}-8x\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,-8):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(2)=\\frac{5}{4}({2}^{4})-8(2)=20-16=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{'} & =-\\frac{1}{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
