        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-342152",
        ":question/problem": "Find the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ at the point $(-1,-3).$\n\n${m}^{'}=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-{x}^{-4}) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+4{x}^{-5} \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(-1,-3):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(-1)=6{(-1)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{(-1)^{5}}=6-4=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{'} & =-\\frac{1}{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
