        ":question/problem": "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{3}-4\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}$ at the point $(1,-3).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)={x}^{3}-4\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-4\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-4{x}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-10{x}^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{\u2032}(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(1)=3(1)^{2}-10(1)^{3/2}=-7\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line  ${m}^{\u2032}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$"
      },
