        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-103543",
        ":question/problem": "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}+7x$ at the point $(1,9).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}+7x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}+7x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-5}+7x) \\\\  & =-10{x}^{-6}+7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{\u2032}(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(1)=-10(1)^{-6}+7=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{\u2032}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"
      },
