        ":question/id": "0b93f0c3-f4cf-5391-8374-d4b6970c54ae",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-71524",
        ":question/problem": "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{2}-5$ at the point $(2,-1).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}-5.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-5)=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{\u2032}(2)=2(2)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{\u2032}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"
